<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tetela people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Batetela</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tetela are a Bantu people of the Sankuru and Maniema regions of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Many served as soldiers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/force-publique">Force Publique</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, and Tetela troops mutinied in 1895 at Luluabourg and in 1897 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-dhanis">Francis Dhanis</a>'s expedition towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, fighting the state for years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a> was a Tetela.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tetela-people.jpg" alt="Tetela people" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StoryoftheCongoFreeState_150b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The peoples of the Kasai Basin up to Maniema understood themselves as descendants of AnKutshu Membele, and many accepted the label Tetela, imposed in colonial times, only in the 20th century. Batetela did not exist as a clan or tribe: it first appears in journals and books between 1885 and 1887, apparently from Watetera, the name for bilingual communities of the Barua lands in Maniema whom Arab slave traders called Utotera. Emil Torday, who studied the peoples of the Congo in 1908 and 1909, was told by a group bearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a> title Sungu that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> had given them the name, and he found it not in general use.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1869-70 Watetera groups came into contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tippu-tip">Tippu Tip</a>, a slave trader from Zanzibar, and the chieftain Gongo Lutete began working with him and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, leading slave raids on the left bank of the Lomami River. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-arab-war">Congo Arab war</a> of 1892-1894 the Watetera went over to the Belgians. The Belgians executed Gongo Lutete in 1893, which has been reported as the cause of the first of the Batetela Rebellions in 1895; uprisings went on until 1908.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a>, the first Prime Minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, assassinated in 1961, was a Tetela. The researcher Ludo De Witte suggested that the scattering of the Batetela across several provinces helped shape the nationalism of figures such as Lumumba. In 1975 many Batetela were purged from the military.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Today the Batetela live between Lusambo and the Upper Congo River, in the provinces of Sankuru and Maniema, by hunting and gathering, fishing and farming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, bananas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a>; many count as subgroups of the larger Mongo group. Most speak the Tetela language; the Methodist Episcopal Church South founded a mission at Wembo-Nyama in 1910, and from 1914 Daniel and Edith Mumpower recorded the Otetela language. They have a large set of proverbs and use three drums: the ngomo for dancing, the six-toned lukumbi slit drum, used for poetry and messages, and the small ekuli, once a signal of victory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tetela people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Resistance continued, including mutinies of <b>Batetela</b> soldiers in 1895 and 1897 and long wars with Zande, Yaka and other peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
